[
    {
        "content": "<p>The proposition </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>is well-formed. Can it be proven?</p>",
        "id": 580712238,
        "sender_full_name": "Lauri Oksanen",
        "timestamp": 1774027236
    },
    {
        "content": "<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">congr</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">card</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">at</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">this</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 580712561,
        "sender_full_name": "Kyle Miller",
        "timestamp": 1774027339
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>They have different cardinalities — I told it to compute whether <code>Nat.card Prop = Nat.card Nat</code>. The former is 2, the latter is 0 (since I used <code>Nat.card</code>, which is 0 for infinite types; I could have reached for other cardinality functions too).</p>",
        "id": 580712772,
        "sender_full_name": "Kyle Miller",
        "timestamp": 1774027396
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Note that this is basically the only thing you can do to prove that two types are not equal. If they have the same cardinality then the result is most likely undecidable.</p>",
        "id": 580712995,
        "sender_full_name": "Riccardo Brasca",
        "timestamp": 1774027473
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Is it undecidable or simply not well-defined?</p>",
        "id": 580717122,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1774028793
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>or maybe undecidable in the ZF model of <a href=\"https://github.com/digama0/lean-type-theory/releases/tag/v1.0\">#leantt</a> ?</p>",
        "id": 580717180,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1774028811
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It is surely well defined</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℤ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>And in practice it can't be proven (I think), nor its negation can.</p>",
        "id": 580717607,
        "sender_full_name": "Riccardo Brasca",
        "timestamp": 1774028951
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Okay, the type theory doesn't provide a way to prove or disprove this equality outside cardinality arguments. This is not about computational decidability right?</p>",
        "id": 580719296,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1774029548
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I am not qualified to answer (I don't know what \"computational decidability means).</p>",
        "id": 580721525,
        "sender_full_name": "Riccardo Brasca",
        "timestamp": 1774030295
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I mean what we call decidability in the context of Turing machines and all that.</p>",
        "id": 580721778,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1774030377
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think the correct word here is \"independent\", this is independent of Lean's type theory with the standard three <code>axiom</code>s.</p>",
        "id": 580721951,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1774030444
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks. That word somehow eluded me until you said it.</p>",
        "id": 580722111,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1774030505
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>\"Undecidable\" is an accepted word for \"independent\".</p>",
        "id": 580724541,
        "sender_full_name": "Kyle Miller",
        "timestamp": 1774031463
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>it's better to use \"independent\" because \"undecidable\" also has the meaning of \"a set whose membership is not <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ComputablePred#doc\">docs#ComputablePred</a>\"</p>",
        "id": 580724865,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1774031567
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh wow, what is this <code>congr(...)</code> magic <span aria-label=\"slight smile\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f642\" role=\"img\" title=\"slight smile\">:slight_smile:</span> ? I would just use <code>apply_fun</code></p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">apply_fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">card</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">at</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">at</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 580823686,
        "sender_full_name": "Mirek Olšák",
        "timestamp": 1774103339
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>congr() is fun because you can apply more complicated pattern</p>",
        "id": 580827102,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1774106146
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks a lot for all the replies. Perhaps </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">zero</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat'</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">succ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat'</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>is independent of Lean's type theory with the standard axioms?</p>",
        "id": 581056985,
        "sender_full_name": "Lauri Oksanen",
        "timestamp": 1774258108
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes. Equality for Lean has nothing special, there is no philosophical meaning behind it (it has actually a 100% standard definition), and for types this property is usually very difficult to prove, unless two types are equal by definition.</p>",
        "id": 581058356,
        "sender_full_name": "Riccardo Brasca",
        "timestamp": 1774258458
    }
]